WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.002
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.302 --> 00:00:08.711
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.011 --> 00:00:15.518
Unit three: Advanced Negotiation Theory. Today you will analyse deals using technical nominalization.

4
00:00:15.818 --> 00:00:18.680
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:22.797
Part one: words. You will learn ten new words.

6
00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:24.442
BATNA.

7
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:28.866
BATNA means your best alternative to a negotiated agreement.

8
00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:32.425
Her strong BATNA let her walk away from the first offer.

9
00:00:33.017 --> 00:00:35.239
Your turn. Say: BATNA.

10
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:38.915
BATNA.

11
00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.429
Zone of possible agreement.

12
00:00:42.417 --> 00:00:46.552
Zone of possible agreement means the range in which both sides can accept a deal.

13
00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:51.170
No zone of possible agreement existed, so talks collapsed.

14
00:00:51.783 --> 00:00:55.074
Your turn. Say: Zone of possible agreement.

15
00:00:57.933 --> 00:00:59.886
Zone of possible agreement.

16
00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:02.011
Integrative bargaining.

17
00:01:03.017 --> 00:01:07.443
Integrative bargaining means negotiating to create value that benefits both parties.

18
00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:11.396
Integrative bargaining turned a dispute into a partnership.

19
00:01:11.983 --> 00:01:15.088
Your turn. Say: Integrative bargaining.

20
00:01:17.933 --> 00:01:19.515
Integrative bargaining.

21
00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:21.657
Distributive bargaining.

22
00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:28.098
Distributive bargaining means negotiating over a fixed amount where one side's gain is the other's loss.

23
00:01:28.683 --> 00:01:31.240
Haggling over a price is distributive bargaining.

24
00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:34.642
Your turn. Say: Distributive bargaining.

25
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.267
Distributive bargaining.

26
00:01:40.017 --> 00:01:40.867
Anchoring.

27
00:01:41.883 --> 00:01:46.158
Anchoring means setting a first number that influences all later offers.

28
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.702
Anchoring high shifted the whole negotiation upward.

29
00:01:50.317 --> 00:01:52.876
Your turn. Say: Anchoring.

30
00:01:55.733 --> 00:01:56.549
Anchoring.

31
00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:58.830
Reservation price.

32
00:01:59.817 --> 00:02:03.964
Reservation price means the worst deal a party will accept before walking away.

33
00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:08.047
The buyer's reservation price was well below the asking price.

34
00:02:08.650 --> 00:02:11.465
Your turn. Say: Reservation price.

35
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.300
Reservation price.

36
00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:18.248
Value creation.

37
00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:23.061
Value creation means finding trades that make both sides better off.

38
00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:27.287
Value creation required understanding what each side truly needed.

39
00:02:27.883 --> 00:02:31.116
Your turn. Say: Value creation.

40
00:02:33.967 --> 00:02:35.188
Value creation.

41
00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:37.741
Negotiation leverage.

42
00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:43.176
Negotiation leverage means the power to influence the outcome of a negotiation.

43
00:02:43.783 --> 00:02:47.257
A rival bid gave her considerable negotiation leverage.

44
00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:51.269
Your turn. Say: Negotiation leverage.

45
00:02:54.133 --> 00:02:55.854
Negotiation leverage.

46
00:02:56.617 --> 00:02:57.745
Counteroffer.

47
00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:02.224
Counteroffer means a new proposal made in response to an offer.

48
00:03:02.817 --> 00:03:05.606
He rejected the terms and submitted a counteroffer.

49
00:03:06.217 --> 00:03:08.927
Your turn. Say: Counteroffer.

50
00:03:11.767 --> 00:03:12.826
Counteroffer.

51
00:03:13.583 --> 00:03:14.410
Deadlock.

52
00:03:15.417 --> 00:03:19.100
Deadlock means a situation in which no progress is possible.

53
00:03:19.683 --> 00:03:22.647
The talks reached a deadlock over a single clause.

54
00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.007
Your turn. Say: Deadlock.

55
00:03:28.867 --> 00:03:29.798
Deadlock.

56
00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:34.274
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

57
00:03:35.117 --> 00:03:40.021
Visal, we lost the contract talks. Their final offer was simply too low.

58
00:03:41.117 --> 00:03:47.298
Did you consider your BATNA before the meeting? Without a credible alternative, your leverage was limited.

59
00:03:48.383 --> 00:03:51.358
We focused on price and ignored everything else.

60
00:03:52.450 --> 00:04:03.531
That was the mistake. Treating the deal as distributive bargaining overlooked opportunities for value creation through delivery terms and payment schedules.

61
00:04:04.617 --> 00:04:07.255
So the negotiation could have been expanded?

62
00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:15.879
Precisely. The expansion of the issues often widens the zone of possible agreement and reduces the risk of deadlock.

63
00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:18.487
Part three: grammar.

64
00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:22.371
Grammar focus: Technical nominalization.

65
00:04:22.671 --> 00:04:23.893
Look at the pattern.

66
00:04:24.583 --> 00:04:27.802
Value creation depends on understanding each party's BATNA.

67
00:04:28.717 --> 00:04:31.982
The expansion of issues reduces the risk of deadlock.

68
00:04:32.883 --> 00:04:36.044
Early anchoring strengthens a negotiator's leverage.

69
00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:49.126
Turn actions into nouns for a formal, technical register: negotiate → negotiation, expand → expansion, anchor → anchoring. The noun phrase becomes the subject of the sentence.

70
00:04:49.883 --> 00:04:54.823
Be careful! Don't say: Value creation depends on understand BATNA.

71
00:04:56.767 --> 00:05:00.508
Say: Value creation depends on understanding BATNA.

72
00:05:00.808 --> 00:05:03.214
After a preposition use a noun or -ing.

73
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.815
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

74
00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:09.344
The Architecture of a Deal.

75
00:05:09.644 --> 00:05:19.608
Negotiation theory distinguishes between distributive bargaining, in which a fixed resource is divided, and integrative bargaining, in which the available value is expanded.

76
00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:28.939
Skilled negotiators therefore begin by assessing their BATNA, because the strength of an alternative determines how much leverage they genuinely possess.

77
00:05:29.933 --> 00:05:37.006
Anchoring, the practice of making the first offer, can shape the entire subsequent discussion, even when the figure is arbitrary.

78
00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:44.934
The identification of shared interests widens the zone of possible agreement, whereas rigid positions make deadlock more likely.

79
00:05:45.933 --> 00:05:52.298
Value creation, not victory over the other side, is consequently regarded as the mark of expert negotiation.

80
00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:57.332
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

81
00:05:58.183 --> 00:06:00.740
The blank of issues widens the zone of agreement.

82
00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:04.752
A, expand. B, expansion. C, expanded.

83
00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.183
The answer is B: expansion.

84
00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:14.963
Your best alternative to a negotiated agreement is your blank.

85
00:06:15.263 --> 00:06:19.265
A, BATNA. B, anchor. C, counteroffer.

86
00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:24.416
The answer is A: BATNA.

87
00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:28.467
Setting a first number that shapes later offers is blank.

88
00:06:28.767 --> 00:06:32.049
A, deadlock. B, anchoring. C, leverage.

89
00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.437
The answer is B: anchoring.

90
00:06:38.583 --> 00:06:39.863
Which is more formal?

91
00:06:40.163 --> 00:06:49.180
A, We negotiate well when we understand needs.. B, Effective negotiation depends on understanding needs.. C, Good negotiating is when you know needs..

92
00:06:52.733 --> 00:06:57.066
The answer is B: Effective negotiation depends on understanding needs..

93
00:06:58.217 --> 00:07:01.795
According to the reading, expert negotiation is marked by…

94
00:07:02.095 --> 00:07:07.676
A, victory over the other side. B, rigid positions. C, value creation.

95
00:07:11.233 --> 00:07:13.384
The answer is C: value creation.

96
00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:17.682
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

97
00:07:18.517 --> 00:07:22.687
What negotiation strategy do you find most effective in complex deals?

98
00:07:39.483 --> 00:07:40.914
Now listen to Visal.

99
00:07:41.214 --> 00:08:13.108
In complex deals I find integrative bargaining the most effective strategy, because it enlarges the pie instead of fighting over its size. Before any meeting, I clarify my BATNA and try to identify the other side's priorities. Often what matters to them costs me little, and vice versa. By trading across several issues, we widen the zone of possible agreement. This approach also preserves the relationship, which is valuable when the parties must work together afterwards.

100
00:08:13.408 --> 00:08:15.071
Excellent answer, Visal!

101
00:08:15.983 --> 00:08:20.784
Great work! Now you can use ten new words and technical nominalization.

102
00:08:21.084 --> 00:08:26.395
Today's value is principled negotiation. Seek agreements that serve both sides fairly.

103
00:08:26.695 --> 00:08:29.370
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

104
00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:32.915
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

